# A blueprint value made under a tag's arm and applied by a block: a plain function
# matches an element and gives the blueprint of a widget that answers it, inside an
# option; the block takes it out, applies it and binds its plain result (Semantics ch. 9,
# "How a supplier answers": under a tag's arm a blueprint answers with that tag's output;
# ch. 8: "a blueprint builds nothing until a block applies it", at the applying block's
# next positions; `<-` builds the child and binds its result; Type System ch. 2: a
# blueprint is an opaque handle and still a value). Written by Claude on the
# claude-lowering branch; subject to change.
emit Ui.*
test mod Tests
use Ui.*

tag Pad.Ask of Element Text:
    name: Text

tag Pad.Skip of Element Nat:
    name: Text

@Answerer: fn Text -> @Text
fn @Answerer name:
    Ui.Label($text=$"answering {name}")                         # 0
    @ "yes {name}"

?Offer: fn Element Pad.* o -> ?@o
fn ?Offer e = match e:
    Pad.Ask(name=n): ? @Answerer n
    Pad.Skip(..): nil

@page: @unit
@page:
    match ?Offer (Pad.Ask(name="a")):                           # 0 and 1
        ? @t:
            heard <- @t
            Ui.Label($text=$"heard {heard}")
        nil: Ui.Label($text=$"none")
    Ui.Label($text=$"end")                                      # 2
